{"id": "c92da7d9-19a8-42c6-b361-50b23ee94b22", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:38:36.438528", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "ea65ee4b-f900-46a0-aeb2-df463195858a", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: The repository at /workspace contains only task.json. No zero-bubble pipeline parallelism code, no simulator, no torch. The environment note says \"project dependencies are not preinstalled.\" task.json says \"Design-only task. Repository and computation resources are not required.\"\n\nTherefore, my main finding in the investigation is: the claimed repository code does not exist in this masked environment. All references to specific files, classes, and functions cannot be verified from the repository. I need to be honest: I cannot enumerate the actual code paths for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p, because no such code exists in /workspace.\n\nWhat I can do: report the verified absence, describe what such a repository would typically be (e.g., a \"pipebench\" simulator, or a from-scratch reimplementation based on the ZB-HYB paper from the \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\" paper, Bytedance/zero_bubble_pipelined_parallelism on GitHub), and then propose a plan skeleton based on a design that either defines a minimal simulator interface as an assumption, or requests that the researcher provide repository access.\n\nThe researcher requested a check-only pass: three deliverables (inspection, plan skeleton, assumptions). The required handoff stage is \"plan.\" Therefore, I should end the turn with stage=plan, presenting the following:\n\nI. Inspection findings (with honest negative results):\n1. /workspace contains only task.json (verified by ls, find, grep). No .py files, no README, no scheduler implementations. Zero grep hits for zero bubble/ZB-1p/ZB-2p across all of /workspace. Filesystem-wide searches also found nothing related.\n2. Throughput tools: absent. No simulator, no cost model, no bubble ratio calculation. torch not installed; python 3.14.4; pip list shows only system packages (ansible etc.), no numpy/torch.\n3. Configurable dimensions: none verifiable from the code.\n4. Environment: system Python 3.14, network disabled, no GPU, no sample scripts or unit tests.\n\nII. Proposed design skeleton. Since the repository is empty, the design must be based on self-contained analytical/event-driven scheduler simulation to be built per the task specifications, using the formal definitions from the ZB-Pipe paper (Fan et al. 2021, arXiv:2106.00225): 1F1B, 1F1B interleaved, ZB-1p, ZB-2p. Define the following:\n- Independent variable m ∈ {4, 8, 16, 32, ...} (number of microbatches) — explicitly marked as assumed stages.\n- Dependent variable: throughput via simulated iteration time T(m, method): tokens/s normalized by per-microbatch time unit; auxiliary metrics: bubble ratio = idle time per stage / total time; makespan.\n- Controlled variables: p stages (e.g., 4), L layers evenly partitioned (L = p*k), recompute policy (activation recomputation with r = 1 imaginary microbatch cost for ZB-1p/ZB-2p; none/standard for 1F1B/1F1B-I), per-microbatch unit FWD/BWD cost model (f = b, or b = 2f — an assumption that must be fixed), no memory constraint (or memory parameter fixed).\n- Comparison grid: 4 methods × m ladder; focus on small m tiers {2p, 4p?}... in ZB-2p, memory grows with 2p; ZB-1p requires m >= p...\n- Analysis: monotonic trend test (Spearman correlation of m and throughput per method), plateau detection, head-to-head comparisons at small m.\n- Feasibility: deliver as design document; minimum required inputs if later simulated.\n\nIII. Assumptions list: repository code unavailable → all interface names are design-time constructs, not verified; throughput metric choice; simulated time acceptable; microbatch stages; cost model ratios; memory parameter semantics; etc.\n\nI must distinguish between \"verified in the repository\" and \"suggestion.\" Verified content: absence of everything. Suggestions: everything else, all marked as assumptions based on the paper rather than the repository.\n\nAlso worth noting: the task's question presupposes that the repository implements these methods; in this masked environment that cannot be verified, so the plan should include a step to identify actual entry points once the repository is provided, with the plan parameterized on those interfaces.\n\nLet me do one more thorough check: hidden files elsewhere, archives (tarballs), /tmp, /opt, git objects, etc.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "find / -xdev \\( -name \"*.tar*\" -o -name \"*.zip\" -o -name \"*.whl\" -o -name \"*.egg\" \\) -not -path \"/proc/*\" -not -path \"/sys/*\" -not -path \"/usr/*\" -not -path \"/var/*\" -not -path \"/etc/*\" 2>/dev/null | head; echo ---; ls -laR /tmp /var/tmp 2>/dev/null | head -30; echo ---; find / -xdev -name \".git\" -maxdepth 4 -not -path \"/usr/*\" 2>/dev/null; echo ---; python3 -m pip list 2>/dev/null | grep -i -E \"numpy|torch|pandas|simpy|matplotlib|networkx\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab6578702fe6712b", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab6578702fe6712b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"find / -xdev \\\\( -name \\\"*.tar*\\\" -o -name \\\"*.zip\\\" -o -name \\\"*.whl\\\" -o -name \\\"*.egg\\\" \\\\) -not -path \\\"/proc/*\\\" -not -path \\\"/sys/*\\\" -not -path \\\"/usr/*\\\" -not -path \\\"/var/*\\\" -not -path \\\"/etc/*\\\" 2>/dev/null | head; echo ---; ls -laR /tmp /var/tmp 2>/dev/null | head -30; echo ---; find / -xdev -name \\\".git\\\" -maxdepth 4 -not -path \\\"/usr/*\\\" 2>/dev/null; echo ---; python3 -m pip list 2>/dev/null | grep -i -E \\\"numpy|torch|pandas|simpy|matplotlib|networkx\\\"\", \"summary\": \"Final sweep for archives, git repos, numeric Python packages\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-af3ebd5c-13c2-4fec-ba1d-4eafa5e27048",