ident counts.\n\nFrom the ZB-Pipe paper, per-device memory (number of microbatches whose activations must be held):\n- 1F1B: m − p (approximately, holding m−p+... at steady state? Actually, the maximum number of in-flight activations for 1F1B is p? Hmm.)\n\nStandard result: In GPipe, memory ∝ m. In 1F1B, memory ∝ p (specifically max p at stage 0, stage i holds... at stage i it holds p − i microbatches; max = p). In 1F1B interleaved with s virtual stages, memory ∝ s·p. In ZB-1p, memory ≤ p + 1? The paper says: ZB-1p keeps in-flight backward inputs ≤ p; ZB-2p ≤ 2p. Yes — paper: \"ZB-HYB\"... From the paper's abstract: \"ZB-1p and ZB-2p reduce memory usage to p+1 and 2p...\". Actually: \"ZB-1p achieves zero bubble with memory usage p + 1 and communication volume...\". From my memory, \"ZB-1p uses p+1 memory, ZB-2p uses 2p, 1F1B uses p.\" And 1F1B interleaved is: sp.\n\nThe paper's Table 1 (Section 4?): Memory usage (number of resident activations per device):\n- GPipe: m\n- 1F1B: p\n- 1F1B interleaved: s·p\n- ZB-1p: p + 1\n- ZB-2p: 2p\n\nHere, memory is measured as the number of microbatch activations held simultaneously per device (peak), assuming a chunk size of one stage's layers. The researcher wrote \"ZB-1p ≤ 1 + related term, ZB-2p ≤ 2 + related term,\" which matches p+1 and 2p. And \"1F1B is an m−stage related formula\": hmm — 1F1B memory = p, which can be written as min(m, p) or stage-index dependent; for stage i (i=0..p−1), peak resident count = min(m, p − i). Total per-device peak is at stage 0: min(m, p). I'll write it as: stage i holds at most min(m, p − i) at the steady warmup peak; the max across stages is min(m, p). This is the standard form.\n\nTiming formulas (paper, Section 5 / appendix analysis), letting r be the recompute time per microbatch (imaginary input):\n- 1F1B: T = (m + p − 1)(f + b). Bubble ratio = (p−1)(f+b) / (m(f+b)) = (p−1)/m.\n- 1F1B interleaved (Megatron), factor s: T = ((m + p − 1)(f + b) + (s−1)(f + b)/s·?) Hmm. Megatron paper's for