plementation generates textual schedules for p ≤ 8, m ≤ 2p? The reference simulator generates exact schedules for small p via priority search). For large m, use the paper's structural pattern. In the design document, define T(method, m) := schedule makespan extracted from generated event schedules; cross-check against paper formulas where they exist.\n\nImportant: Do not report numerical results. Present everything as extraction rules / criteria.\n\nAlso the m=1 edge case: 1F1B is feasible (degenerate); ZB schedules typically require m ≥ ...; ZB-2p needs m ≥ ...? Paper constraint: for ZB-1p, m must satisfy m ≥ ...? ZB-2p requires m to be a multiple of ...? Reference simulator supports m and p with constraints like m ≥ p. The design document should state: for m=1, record feasibility or explicit infeasibility note per the scheduler's construction rules.\n\nDocument structure per delivery requirements (i)-(vi). Also include sensitivity analysis per researcher's fixed choices: main is b=2f, comm=0; sensitivity: b=f; non-zero communication; p=8; memory-constrained. For 1F1B-I: full grid + divisibility subset. Report ranking changes faithfully. Include falsification criteria. Include assumption registry classifying each item as literature-based/design-choice. Include minimum input contract table and remapping notes. Include limitations.\n\nAlso the \"peak resident backward count\" formula (per paper's Table/notation, activations measured in microbatch units at stage-level chunk granularity):\n- 1F1B: stage i holds ≤ min(m, p − i); device peak = min(m, p) at stage 0.\n- 1F1B interleaved: s × that: ≤ s·min(m, p−i); peak = s·min(m, p) (Megatron-SP reports memory ∝ s·p).\n- ZB-1p: peak per device ≤ p + 1 (paper claims same as 1F1b + 1); expressed as p+1.\n- ZB-2p: ≤ 2p.\n- (GPipe m for reference.)\nAlso recompute trades r unit compute for reduced storage; report both formula values and peak counts extracted from generated schedules.\n\nNormalization: f = 1 time unit; b = 2; T